Assistant: ## Failing Test: "Admin UI login with openidm-admin succeeds"

The job fails in the **Playwright UI Smoke Tests** step. Specifically, the test at <a href="https://github.com/OpenIdentityPlatform/OpenIDM/blob/bc5b3c7aedd6b441181f2d4f5abe5110581e2627/e2e/ui-smoke-test.spec.mjs#L17">`e2e/ui-smoke-test.spec.mjs:17`</a> — **"Admin UI login with openidm-admin succeeds"** — fails on both the initial run and the retry with:

```
expect(received).toBe(expected) // Object.is equality
Expected: false
Received: true
```

This means the `#login` field is **still visible** after the login attempt, indicating the login didn't actually succeed and the UI remained on the login page.

### Root Cause

The test (lines 21–33) fills in credentials, clicks submit, then waits for a post-login element (`#content`, `.navbar`, or `#wrapper`) to appear. The issue is on **line 23**:

```javascript
await page.click("[type=submit], .btn-primary");
```

The selector `[type=submit], .btn-primary` is a CSS selector list. Playwright's `page.click()` may match the **first** element in the DOM that matches either selector — which might not be the actual login button. If the OpenIDM Admin UI login button doesn't match either of these selectors exactly (or if there's another element matching first), the click has no effect and the login form stays visible.

### Solution

Update the click selector to more precisely target the login button. Inspect what the actual Admin UI renders — it's typically an `input` or `button` inside the login form. A more robust approach:

```javascript
// e2e/ui-smoke-test.spec.mjs - line 17 test
test("Admin UI login with openidm-admin succeeds", async ({ page }) => {
    await page.goto(`${BASE_URL}/admin/`);
    await page.waitForSelector("#login", { timeout: 30000 });

    await page.fill("#login", ADMIN_USER);
    await page.fill("#password", ADMIN_PASS);

    // Use a more targeted selector and wait for navigation/response after click
    await Promise.all([
        page.waitForNavigation({ waitUntil: "networkidle", timeout: 30000 }).catch(() => {}),
        page.click("input[type=submit], button[type=submit], #loginButton, .btn-primary"),
    ]);

    // Give the SPA time to transition
    await page.waitForFunction(() => {
        return document.querySelector("#content") !== null
            || document.querySelector(".navbar") !== null
            || document.querySelector("#wrapper") !== null;
    }, { timeout: 30000 });

    const loginField = await page.$("#login");
    const isLoginVisible = loginField ? await loginField.isVisible() : false;
    expect(isLoginVisible).toBe(false);
});
```

Similarly, apply the same fix to the **dashboard test** (line 67) which uses the same click pattern:

```javascript
// line 72
await page.click("input[type=submit], button[type=submit], #loginButton, .btn-primary");
```

### Alternative / Additional Fix

If the Admin UI login form uses a `<form>` element, you can bypass the button selector entirely and submit the form directly:

```javascript
await page.fill("#login", ADMIN_USER);
await page.fill("#password", ADMIN_PASS);

// Press Enter on the password field to submit
await page.press("#password", "Enter");
```

This is often more reliable for legacy UIs where button selectors can be fragile.

### Summary

| Issue | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Login test fails — `#login` still visible after click | `page.click("[type=submit], .btn-primary")` doesn't click the correct login button | Use a more specific selector (e.g., `input[type=submit]`, `#loginButton`) or use `page.press("#password", "Enter")` to submit the form |

User: make PR

Assistant: I've started a Copilot coding agent session to create a PR that fixes the failing Playwright test. The fix will update `e2e/ui-smoke-test.spec.mjs` on the `copilot/add-playwright-ui-smoke-tests` branch to replace the unreliable `page.click("[type=submit], .btn-primary")` with `page.press("#password", "Enter")` in both the login test and the dashboard test. I'm waiting for your confirmation to proceed.

