&gt; * [x]  Analyze error/secret hygiene
&gt; * [x]  Assess test coverage
&gt; * [x]  Write final review
&gt; 
&gt; ### Review bar for this PR
&gt; * All 8 targeted CodeQL alerts closed on latest head (no suppressions without justification)
&gt; * `validateLLMBaseUrl()` fails closed on malformed input, rejects non-HTTP(S) schemes, restricts `http://` to loopback only
&gt; * `callLLM()` calls `validateLLMBaseUrl()` before any fetch, stream read, or disk write
&gt; * `setBackendUrl()` does not mutate `_backendUrl` before `validateBackendUrl()` passes
&gt; * Log injection: no user-controlled strings reach `console.log` or logger via string interpolation without CRLF sanitization
&gt; * No API keys, credentials, prompts, or bearer tokens in error messages or new log fields
&gt; * Tests would fail before this PR and cover both positive and negative security cases
&gt; * CI, Gitleaks, Dependency Review, and CodeQL are green; no hidden bidi controls in security-critical files
&gt; 
&gt; ### Current PR state
&gt; Item	Status
&gt; Latest head SHA	`6a6e4d8`
&gt; PR state	Open
&gt; Commits	5
&gt; Changed files	6 TypeScript files
&gt; Net diff	+173 / -11
&gt; CodeQL	✅ **SUCCESS**
&gt; Gitleaks	✅ success
&gt; Dependency Review	✅ success
&gt; PR Autofix	✅ success (formatting suggestions posted — see below)
&gt; CI (unit/platform tests)	⏳ in_progress at review time
&gt; Docker Build	⏳ in_progress at review time
&gt; PR Description Check	⚠️ action_required (label automation, not a functional blocker)
&gt; Vercel	Deployed — not materially relevant to this security review
&gt; **CodeQL passed on latest head.** This is the single most important CI gate for this PR: all 8 targeted `js/log-injection`, `js/http-to-file-access`, and `js/client-side-request-forgery` alerts are closed or were not re-raised by the static analysis on `6a6e4d8`.
&gt; 
&gt; ### Branch hygiene assessment
&gt; **Verdict: merge-from-main commit present but harmless and merge-safe — with one squash recommendation.**
&gt; 
&gt; The 5 commits are:
&gt; 
&gt; 1. `1c48ff9 Initial plan` — Copilot planning artifact; adds no code. **Should be squashed before final merge** — it pollutes the permanent history and adds no signal for future bisects.
&gt; 2. `e39ba76 fix(security): U11 ...` — substantive security fix
&gt; 3. `3ddfc45 fix: correct IPv6 loopback check` — follow-up correction (IPv6 bracket stripping)
&gt; 4. `6d131bc fix: also sanitize error message in bridge-db log call` — follow-up correction
&gt; 5. `6a6e4d8 Merge branch 'main' into ...` — pulled in `248cb1e` (`.gitnexusignore` + `--skip-git` regression tests, already on `main`). The merge commit itself introduces no unrelated changes to the PR diff — the 6 changed TypeScript files remain focused on the U11 security remediations.
&gt; 
&gt; No lockfile churn, workflow changes, generated artifacts, Docker changes, or unrelated application changes are present in the PR diff.
&gt; 
&gt; ### Understanding of the change
&gt; **Why CodeQL flags the LLM base URL as `http-to-file-access`:** `config.baseUrl` flows from user-controlled config (env vars, CLI flags, saved JSON) into `buildRequestUrl()` and then `fetch()`. The response body is later written to disk (wiki file). CodeQL traces this as: user-controlled string → `fetch()` URL → HTTP response → disk write. If `file:///etc/passwd` is supplied, `fetch()` on Node ≥18 follows the WHATWG URL spec and can read local files, piping their contents into the wiki output.
&gt; 
&gt; **Why local HTTP is still needed:** Ollama, LiteLLM, and local proxy servers listen on `http://localhost:11434` or `http://127.0.0.1:11434` — they cannot easily run TLS. Blocking `http://` entirely would break common local setups.
&gt; 
&gt; **Why backend-client validation is scheme-only:** The GitNexus web client is designed to connect to any remote GitNexus server, not just localhost. Restricting to loopback would break the product. Scheme-only validation closes the `javascript:`, `file://`, and `data:` forgery paths without constraining legitimate remote server connectivity.
&gt; 
&gt; **Why structured logging + CRLF sanitization closes log injection:** CRLF injection (`\r\n`) in a non-NDJSON log sink can create fake log lines. Pino's JSON serialization already encodes CRLF as `\\r\\n` within JSON values, but CodeQL models the raw string flow; explicit `.replace(/[\r\n]/g, ' ')` before the logger call makes the sanitization visible to both the analyzer and human reviewers.
&gt; 
&gt; **Out of scope:** Full SSRF protection (DNS rebinding, IPv4-mapped IPv6 for HTTPS LLM providers, RFC 1918 IP allowlisting), since HTTPS connections to public LLM providers are intentionally unrestricted. The PR correctly scopes to "break CodeQL taint flow" rather than "implement full SSRF firewall."
&gt; 
&gt; ### Findings
&gt; #### [LOW] URL validation error messages may leak credentials
&gt; * **Category:** Secret/credential hygiene (DoD §2.5)
&gt; * **Files:** `gitnexus/src/core/wiki/llm-client.ts:99,104,114`, `gitnexus-web/src/services/backend-client.ts:220,224`
&gt; * **Issue:** All validation errors include the raw input URL in the message:
&gt;   ```ts
&gt;   // llm-client.ts:99
&gt;   throw new Error(`Invalid LLM base URL: ${baseUrl}`);
&gt;   // llm-client.ts:104
&gt;   throw new Error(`LLM base URL must use http:// or https:// (got ${parsed.protocol}): ${baseUrl}`);
&gt;   // llm-client.ts:114
&gt;   `Use https:// for remote endpoints: ${baseUrl}`
&gt;   // backend-client.ts:220
&gt;   throw new Error(`Invalid backend URL: ${url}`);
&gt;   // backend-client.ts:224
&gt;   `Backend URL must use http:// or https:// (got ${parsed.protocol}): ${url}`
&gt;   ```
&gt;   
&gt;   
&gt;       
&gt;         
&gt;       
&gt;   
&gt;         
&gt;       
&gt;   
&gt;       
&gt;     
&gt;   If a user configures `https://user:sk-abc1234@api.openai.com/v1`, the API key appears verbatim in the thrown `Error.message`, which surfaces in CLI output, stack traces, and potentially server logs.
&gt; * **Why it matters:** DoD §2.5 explicitly states "No secrets, tokens, or credentials committed to the repo, to logs, or to error messages." LLM provider URLs with embedded API keys are a known pattern (some providers document it). CLI error output is often copy-pasted into bug reports.
&gt; * **Recommended fix:** Replace `${baseUrl}` / `${url}` with `${parsed.origin}` or `${parsed.protocol}//${parsed.hostname}` after the successful `new URL()` parse. For the malformed-URL error (where `new URL()` throws), use a safe placeholder:
&gt;   ```ts
&gt;   // Safe: only includes scheme+host, not user:password
&gt;   throw new Error(`LLM base URL must use http:// or https:// (got ${parsed.protocol}): ${parsed.origin}`);
&gt;   ```
&gt;   
&gt;   
&gt;       
&gt;         
&gt;       
&gt;   
&gt;         
&gt;       
&gt;   
&gt;       
&gt;     
