&gt; 
&gt; ### LLM URL validation assessment
&gt; `validateLLMBaseUrl()` at `llm-client.ts:94-119` is well-implemented:
&gt; 
&gt; Check	Result
&gt; Fails closed on malformed URL (`new URL()` throws)	✅
&gt; Rejects any non-`http:`/`https:` protocol	✅
&gt; Allows `https://` for any hostname (OpenAI, Azure, OpenRouter, custom)	✅
&gt; Allows `http://localhost`	✅
&gt; Allows `http://127.0.0.1`	✅
&gt; Allows `http://[::1]` (IPv6 loopback with bracket handling)	✅
&gt; `LOCALHOST` (uppercased) → allowed via `.toLowerCase()`	✅
&gt; `localhost.` (trailing dot) → **rejected** (≠ `localhost`) — conservative but correct	✅
&gt; `localhost.evil.com` → rejected (full hostname comparison, not prefix)	✅
&gt; `127.0.0.1.evil.com` → rejected	✅
&gt; `http://192.168.1.1` → rejected	✅
&gt; `http://0.0.0.0` → rejected	✅
&gt; `http://169.254.169.254` → rejected	✅
&gt; `http://[::ffff:127.0.0.1]` → bracket-stripped to `::ffff:127.0.0.1` ≠ `::1` → rejected	✅
&gt; `http://[fe80::1]` → bracket-stripped to `fe80::1` ≠ `::1` → rejected	✅
&gt; `http://0177.0.0.1` → WHATWG URL normalizes to `127.0.0.1` → **allowed** (correct — it IS loopback)	✅
&gt; `http://2130706433` (decimal IPv4 for 127.0.0.1) → WHATWG normalizes → **allowed** (correct)	✅
&gt; Backslash/encoded-host spoofing not possible — `new URL()` normalizes before comparison	✅
&gt; Does not echo credentials in errors	⚠️ See credential leakage finding above
&gt; The IPv6 bracket-stripping logic (`parsed.hostname.toLowerCase().replace(/^\[|\]$/g, '')`) correctly handles Node's WHATWG URL representation where `http://[::1]/v1` yields `hostname = "[::1]"`.
&gt; 
&gt; ### callLLM integration assessment
&gt; `validateLLMBaseUrl(config.baseUrl)` appears at **line 173**, which is the very first executable statement in `callLLM()` — before message construction, provider detection (`azure` flag), URL building (`buildRequestUrl`), auth header construction, the retry loop, and all `fetch()` calls. The validation is unconditional and not gated on provider type. All paths through `callLLM()` — non-streaming JSON response and streaming SSE — are covered because both use the same `url` from `buildRequestUrl(config.baseUrl, ...)`, and that call is also after validation. Azure content-filter handling, rate-limit retry, and network-error retry paths are all within the retry loop, which is entered only after validation passes. **No fetch-before-validation path exists.**
&gt; 
&gt; ### Backend URL / CSRF assessment
&gt; `setBackendUrl()` at `backend-client.ts:229-233`:
&gt; 
&gt; ```ts
&gt; export const setBackendUrl = (url: string): void =&gt; {
&gt;   const trimmed = url.replace(/\/$/, '');
&gt;   validateBackendUrl(trimmed);   // throws if invalid
&gt;   _backendUrl = trimmed;         // only reached if validation passes
&gt; };
&gt; ```
&gt; 
&gt; Mutation ordering is **correct**: `_backendUrl` is only assigned after `validateBackendUrl(trimmed)` returns normally. If validation throws, `_backendUrl` retains its previous value. This is the correct fail-safe pattern.
&gt; 
&gt; `validateBackendUrl()` correctly:
&gt; 
&gt; * Rejects malformed URLs via `new URL()` exception
&gt; * Rejects `javascript:`, `file:`, `data:`, `blob:`, `ftp:`, `ws:`, `wss:`, and any non-`http:`/`https:` scheme
&gt; * Accepts any `http://` or `https://` URL intentionally (remote server support)
&gt; * Does not restrict to loopback (correct for this use case)
&gt; 
&gt; `normalizeServerUrl()` adds protocol before calling `setBackendUrl()`; this does not bypass validation since the normalized URL is still passed through `validateBackendUrl()` inside `setBackendUrl()`. All downstream fetch calls (`fetchGraph`, `fetchRepos`, `streamSSE`, etc.) use `_backendUrl` as a base prefix — no additional validation is needed since the module-level state is always validated before storage.
&gt; 
&gt; ### Log-injection assessment
&gt; **`bridge-db.ts`:** The diff shows the old call was:
&gt; 
&gt; ```ts
&gt; bridgeLogger.debug({ groupDir, err: lastErr, attempts: ... }, '...');
&gt; ```
&gt; 
&gt; The new call is:
&gt; 
&gt; ```ts
&gt; const safeGroupDir = String(groupDir).replace(/[\r\n]/g, ' ');
&gt; const safeErrMsg = lastErr instanceof Error
&gt;   ? String(lastErr.message).replace(/[\r\n]/g, ' ')
&gt;   : undefined;
&gt; bridgeLogger.debug({ groupDir: safeGroupDir, errMsg: safeErrMsg, attempts: ... }, '...');
&gt; ```
&gt; 
&gt; * Raw `groupDir` no longer logged ✅
&gt; * Raw `err: lastErr` (with unsanitized `.message` and `.stack`) no longer logged ✅ — replaced by only the sanitized message string
&gt; * Both `\r` and `\n` stripped ✅
&gt; * U+2028/U+2029: Pino's NDJSON serializer JSON-encodes these as `\u2028`/`\u2029` within string values; they cannot inject line breaks into NDJSON output regardless. Combined with the CRLF strip, this is adequate for all common log sinks.
&gt; * Some diagnostic loss: full stack trace no longer logged in this path. Acceptable — this fires only when the bridge DB open gave up after 10 retries (already a significant failure), and the error message alone is sufficient to diagnose transient lock races.
&gt; 
&gt; **`api.ts`:** Both `console.log(...)` string-interpolation calls in `resolveRepo` are fully replaced by `logger.debug(...)` with structured fields. Sanitization uses the same `String(x).replace(/[\r\n]/g, ' ')` pattern consistently. Both replacements remain guarded by `if (process.env.DEBUG)`. The `logger` import was already present. No accidental control-flow change introduced.
&gt; 
&gt; ### Error / secret hygiene assessment
&gt; The main concern is the URL credential leakage described in Finding 1 above. Beyond that:
&gt; 
&gt; * No API keys, bearer tokens, or prompts appear in new log fields
&gt; * `safeErrMsg` in bridge-db is only the error message string, not the full error object
&gt; * The `bridgeLogger.debug` structured fields (`groupDir`, `errMsg`, `attempts`) do not include network addresses, file contents, or user data beyond the path
&gt; * `api.ts` structured fields (`jobId`, `repoName`) are sanitized strings — no tokens or prompts
&gt; 
&gt; ### CodeQL / GHAS assessment
&gt; **CodeQL passed (success) on the latest head `6a6e4d8`.** All 8 targeted alerts are closed. The structural approach taken is correct for CodeQL taint-flow analysis:
&gt; 
&gt; * `validateLLMBaseUrl()` throws unconditionally on invalid input; any path through `callLLM()` that passes validation guarantees the URL is `http:` (loopback only) or `https:`. CodeQL can see this as a guard because the throw happens before any sink (`fetch()`).
&gt; * `validateBackendUrl()` throws before `_backendUrl` is mutated; CodeQL tracks the assignment after the guard.
&gt; * Bridge-db and api.ts CRLF sanitization produces new strings via `.replace()` before passing to the logger; CodeQL sees the tainted value is not directly used.
&gt; 
&gt; No new CodeQL alerts introduced. Gitleaks clean. Dependency Review clean.
&gt; 
&gt; ### Test assessment
&gt; **`wiki-llm-client.test.ts`** (new `validateLLMBaseUrl` block, lines 335–379):
&gt; 
&gt; * ✅ `https://api.openai.com/v1`, `https://openrouter.ai`, Azure URL — valid HTTPS passes
&gt; * ✅ `http://localhost:11434/v1`, `http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1`, `http://[::1]:11434/v1` — loopback HTTP passes
&gt; * ✅ `http://evil.example.com`, `http://192.168.1.1` — non-loopback HTTP rejected
&gt; * ✅ `file:///etc/passwd`, `javascript:alert(1)`, `data:text/plain,evil` — non-HTTP rejected
&gt; * ✅ Malformed URL rejected
&gt; * ✅ `callLLM` with `file:///etc/passwd` throws **before** `fetch` (no `vi.stubGlobal('fetch')` needed — the mock global is not called)
&gt; * ❌ Missing: `LOCALHOST`, `ftp://`, `http://10.0.0.1`, `http://169.254.169.254`, `localhost.evil.com`, `127.0.0.1.evil.com`, `localhost.` (trailing dot), `http://[fe80::1]`, `http://[::ffff:127.0.0.1]`
&gt; 
&gt; **`server-connection.test.ts`** (new `validateBackendUrl` / `setBackendUrl` blocks, lines 169–204):
&gt; 
&gt; * ✅ Local HTTP/HTTPS and remote HTTPS accepted
&gt; * ✅ `javascript:`, `file:`, `data:` rejected by both `validateBackendUrl` and `setBackendUrl`
&gt; * ✅ Malformed URL rejected
&gt; * ❌ No test verifies `_backendUrl` is unchanged after `setBackendUrl` rejects (code is correct, test is incomplete — see Finding 2)
&gt; 
&gt; Tests for bridge-db CRLF sanitization are not present in the test files changed by this PR. Bridge-db tests reportedly pass (30/30 per PR comments), but CRLF-injection tests don't appear to be included. This is acceptable given CodeQL confirmed the path is closed, but is a gap.
&gt; 
&gt; ### Hidden Unicode / hygiene assessment
&gt; Non-ASCII characters are present in all changed files, but exclusively as **visible decorative punctuation in comments**: em-dash (—), ellipsis (…), arrow (→), multiply (×), en-dash (–). These are consistent with the existing codebase style (the unchanged portions of the same files use the same characters).
&gt; 
&gt; * ✅ No bidirectional override characters (U+202A–U+202E, U+2066–U+2069)
&gt; * ✅ No zero-width characters, non-breaking spaces in executable code, or homoglyphs in identifiers
&gt; * ✅ No non-ASCII characters in regex patterns, URL strings, error messages, or security comments
&gt; * ✅ No non-ASCII in test assertions or test names
&gt; 
&gt; The GitHub "hidden Unicode" warning on this PR is a false positive for this codebase style — the characters are in pre-existing comments, not in the security-critical new code added by this PR.
&gt; 
&gt; ### Elegance / maintainability assessment
&gt; The implementation is appropriately local and minimal:
&gt; 
&gt; * `validateLLMBaseUrl()` is ~25 lines, pure, easy to test in isolation, co-located with the code it protects
&gt; * `validateBackendUrl()` is ~10 lines, same pattern, consistent with the LLM validator
&gt; * CRLF sanitization is a one-liner `.replace(/[\r\n]/g, ' ')` — no unnecessary abstraction
&gt; * Comments explain the CodeQL context without narrating the code itself
&gt; * No new dependencies introduced
&gt; * No over-engineering: no shared "URL sanitizer" class, no elaborate allow/deny config, no middleware layer
&gt; 
&gt; One style note: `bridge-db.ts` uses a multi-line ternary for `safeErrMsg` (lines 730-732) which is slightly inconsistent with the one-liner pattern used in `api.ts`. This is cosmetic only.
&gt; 
&gt; ### Final verdict
&gt; **production-ready with minor follow-ups**
&gt; 
&gt; The security fundamentals of this PR are sound and correctly implemented. `validateLLMBaseUrl()` is called unconditionally before any fetch, handles all relevant loopback edge cases, and correctly rejects non-HTTP schemes and non-loopback HTTP hosts. `setBackendUrl()` validates before mutating global state. Log injection fixes properly replace string-interpolated console calls with structured logger fields containing sanitized values, and no raw error object is passed to the logger. **CodeQL passed on the latest head**, confirming all 8 targeted alerts are closed without suppression.
&gt; 
&gt; The three follow-ups that should be addressed before or shortly after merge are: (1) redact credentials from URL validation error messages by using `parsed.origin` instead of the raw URL, (2) add a test that proves `_backendUrl` is unchanged after `setBackendUrl()` rejects, and (3) apply the PR Autofix formatting suggestions. The "Initial plan" commit should be squashed. None of these are functional security blockers — the change safely remediates the targeted vulnerability surface.

