familiarize with these ideas

Issue: Multi-channel ("shared-grid") interpolation support

## Motivation

At least one downstream consumer (neopdf) has several independent scalar tables defined on the *same* coordinate grid: PDF flavors all sampled on the same (x, Q²) knots, similar in spirit to an RGB image where each color channel shares the same pixel grid. This pattern seems general enough to support directly, rather than leaving it to be reimplemented per consumer. Evaluating each channel through today's single-channel `Strategy2D`/`Strategy3D` API means re-running the search for the surrounding grid points once per channel, even though that search only depends on the query point and the shared grid, not on the channel's values.

Today, achieving this requires hand-rolling an entirely separate, ninterp-unaware struct per use case (duplicating grid storage, duplicating that search, duplicating the per-point calculation), with no code shared between strategy families.

## Additions

### 1. `find_interval`, a helper built on the existing `find_nearest_index`

```rust
/// Locates `target` within `arr`, returning both the index (as given by
/// `find_nearest_index`) and the fractional position within the interval
/// between that grid point and the next one, in `[0.0, 1.0]` for
/// in-bounds queries.
///
/// No bounds/extrapolation handling is performed here, consistent with
/// `find_nearest_index`; extrapolation policy is resolved upstream via
/// `Extrapolate` before this is ever called.
pub fn find_interval<T>(arr: ArrayView1<T>, target: &T) -> (usize, f64)
where
    T: PartialOrd + Copy + Into<f64>,
{
    let index = find_nearest_index(arr, target);
    let lo: f64 = arr[index].into();
    let hi: f64 = arr[index + 1].into();
    let value: f64 = (*target).into();
    (index, (value - lo) / (hi - lo))
}
```

### 2. `InterpData2DMulti` / `InterpData3DMulti`, concrete sibling containers

Not a generalization of `InterpData<D, N>` (would require const-generic `N+1` arithmetic and would force every single-channel call site to carry an unused channel axis). Instead, these are concrete hand-rolled siblings, matching the crate's existing `one`/`two`/`three`-module pattern.

```rust
/// Multi-channel interpolator data for 2D grids: values on a shared 2D grid,
/// stacked along a third channel axis.
pub struct InterpData2DMulti<D>
where
    D: Data + RawDataClone + Clone,
{
    /// Shared coordinate grid, identical role to `InterpData2D::grid`.
    pub grid: [ArrayBase<D, Ix1>; 2],
    /// Function values: shape `[nx, ny, n_channels]`.
    pub values: ArrayBase<D, Ix3>,
}

impl<D> InterpData2DMulti<D>
where
    D: Data + RawDataClone + Clone,
    D::Elem: PartialOrd + Debug,
{
    /// Validates grid monotonicity/shape (same checks as `InterpData2D`),
    /// plus a non-empty check on the channel axis (which has no `grid`
    /// counterpart to validate shape against).
    pub fn validate(&self) -> Result<(), ValidateError> {
        for i in 0..2 {
            let grid_len = self.grid[i].len();
            if grid_len == 0 {
                return Err(ValidateError::EmptyGrid(i));
            }
            if !self.grid[i].windows(2).into_iter().all(|w| w[0] <= w[1]) {
                return Err(ValidateError::Monotonicity(i));
            }
            if grid_len != self.values.shape()[i] {
                return Err(ValidateError::IncompatibleShapes(i));
            }
        }
        if self.values.shape()[2] == 0 {
            return Err(ValidateError::EmptyGrid(2));
        }
        Ok(())
    }

    pub fn n_channels(&self) -> usize {
        self.values.shape()[2]
    }
}

/// Same relationship to `InterpData3D` as `InterpData2DMulti` has to `InterpData2D`.
pub struct InterpData3DMulti<D>
where
    D: Data + RawDataClone + Clone,
{
    pub grid: [ArrayBase<D, Ix1>; 3],
    pub values: ArrayBase<D, Ix4>,  // [nx, ny, nz, n_channels]
}
```

### 3. `StrategyMulti2D` / `StrategyMulti3D`, new traits rather than shared methods

Kept separate from `Strategy2D::interpolate -> f64` deliberately: unifying into one method signature would force the scalar path to return `Vec<f64>` (a heap allocation) on every call, taxing the common single-channel case to serve the rare multi-channel one.

```rust
pub trait StrategyMulti2D<D> {
    fn interpolate_multi(&self, data: &InterpData2DMulti<D>, point: &[f64; 2])
        -> Result<Vec<f64>, InterpolateError>;
}

pub trait StrategyMulti3D<D> {
    fn interpolate_multi(&self, data: &InterpData3DMulti<D>, point: &[f64; 3])
        -> Result<Vec<f64>, InterpolateError>;
}
```

### 4. Implementations: shared search, computed once per axis

```rust
impl<D> StrategyMulti2D<D> for Linear
where
    D: Data<Elem = f64>,
{
    fn interpolate_multi(&self, data: &InterpData2DMulti<D>, point: &[f64; 2])
        -> Result<Vec<f64>, InterpolateError>
    {
        // Located ONCE per axis, reused across every channel below.
        let (ix, tx) = find_interval(data.grid[0].view(), &point[0]);
        let (iy, ty) = find_interval(data.grid[1].view(), &point[1]);

        let n = data.n_channels();
        let mut out = Vec::with_capacity(n);
        for k in 0..n {
            let v00 = data.values[[ix,     iy,     k]];
            let v10 = data.values[[ix + 1, iy,     k]];
            let v01 = data.values[[ix,     iy + 1, k]];
            let v11 = data.values[[ix + 1, iy + 1, k]];
            let v0 = v00 * (1.0 - tx) + v10 * tx;
            let v1 = v01 * (1.0 - tx) + v11 * tx;
            out.push(v0 * (1.0 - ty) + v1 * ty);
        }
        Ok(out)
    }
}

/// Default-style fallback pattern for strategies with no custom sharing
/// logic: just loops the existing scalar `interpolate` once per channel.
/// Zero speedup, but zero extra implementation burden; strategies only
/// override this if they want the real sharing benefit above.
impl<D> StrategyMulti2D<D> for SomeExistingScalarStrategy
where
    D: Data<Elem = f64>,
{
    fn interpolate_multi(&self, data: &InterpData2DMulti<D>, point: &[f64; 2])
        -> Result<Vec<f64>, InterpolateError>
    {
        (0..data.n_channels())
            .map(|k| {
                let single = InterpData2D {
                    grid: data.grid.clone(),
                    values: data.values.index_axis(Axis(2), k).to_owned(),
                };
                self.interpolate(&single, point)
            })
            .collect()
    }
}
```

### 5. `Interp2DMulti` wrapper (mirrors `Interp2D`)

```rust
pub struct Interp2DMulti<D, S> {
    data: InterpData2DMulti<D>,
    strategy: S,
    extrapolate: Extrapolate,
}

impl<D, S: StrategyMulti2D<D>> Interp2DMulti<D, S> {
    pub fn new(data: InterpData2DMulti<D>, strategy: S, extrapolate: Extrapolate)
        -> Result<Self, ValidateError>
    {
        data.validate()?;
        Ok(Self { data, strategy, extrapolate })
    }

    pub fn interpolate_multi(&self, point: &[f64; 2]) -> Result<Vec<f64>, InterpolateError> {
        self.strategy.interpolate_multi(&self.data, point)
    }
}
```

### 6. `DynInterpolator` / `DynMultiInterpolator`, dyn-compatible traits

`Send + Sync` bound is required at the trait level (not left to be inferred), because auto-trait derivation doesn't reach through `dyn Trait`. `Box<dyn DynInterpolator>` is only `Send + Sync` if the trait itself declares it, even though every real strategy implementor (stateless, read-only math) naturally satisfies it for free.

Named `interpolate_point` / `interpolate_multi` (not `interpolate`) to avoid colliding with an existing `Interpolator::interpolate` trait method that would otherwise require fully-qualified call syntax at every use site.

```rust
pub trait DynInterpolator: Send + Sync {
    fn interpolate_point(&self, point: &[f64]) -> Result<f64, InterpolateError>;
    fn as_any(&self) -> &dyn Any;
}

pub trait DynMultiInterpolator: Send + Sync {
    fn interpolate_multi(&self, point: &[f64]) -> Result<Vec<f64>, InterpolateError>;
    fn as_any(&self) -> &dyn Any;
}

impl<S> DynInterpolator for Interp2DOwned<f64, S>
where
    S: Strategy2D<OwnedRepr<f64>> + 'static + Clone + Send + Sync,
{
    fn interpolate_point(&self, point: &[f64]) -> Result<f64, InterpolateError> {
        let [x, y] = point.try_into()
            .map_err(|_| InterpolateError::Other("Expected 2D point".into()))?;
        self.interpolate(&[x, y])
    }
    fn as_any(&self) -> &dyn Any { self }
}

impl<S> DynMultiInterpolator for Interp2DMultiOwned<f64, S>
where
    S: StrategyMulti2D<OwnedRepr<f64>> + 'static + Clone + Send + Sync,
{
    fn interpolate_multi(&self, point: &[f64]) -> Result<Vec<f64>, InterpolateError> {
        let [x, y] = point.try_into()
            .map_err(|_| InterpolateError::Other("Expected 2D point".into()))?;
        self.interpolate_multi(&[x, y])
    }
    fn as_any(&self) -> &dyn Any { self }
}
```

## Non-goals
- No change to `InterpData<D, N>`'s shape contract or `validate()`.
- No unification of scalar and multi-channel trait methods under one signature.
- No const-generic channel-count parameter; channel count is a runtime axis size, not a type parameter.

---

---

# Issue: Unchecked fast-path interpolation

## Motivation

The default `interpolate()` path does useful safety work on every call: checks whether the point is in bounds, resolves `Extrapolate` policy, and wraps the result in `Result<f64, InterpolateError>`. For hot loops calling the same, already-validated interpolator many times, that overhead is measurable and has driven at least one downstream consumer to abandon this crate's strategy system entirely and hand-write a parallel, unsafe-by-convention fast path outside of it, reimplementing the interpolation math itself in the process, not just skipping the checks.

Goal: give consumers a sanctioned reason to stay inside this crate's type system for this optimization, instead of forcing a choice between "safe but slower" and "fast but you have to leave the crate."

## Design

**Not `unsafe fn`.** Misuse here produces a wrong number or a panic, not memory unsafety, so a documented logic-error contract is the right fit, not Rust's `unsafe` soundness contract (contrast with e.g. `NonZeroU32::new_unchecked`, where misuse is real UB).

**Default-implemented**, so adding this to the trait costs existing implementors nothing; it's opt-in for strategies that specifically want the speedup.

**Splits at the top only**: the actual interpolation math (locating the interval and combining values) is written once and shared; only the thin "check bounds first or don't" wrapper differs between the safe and fast entry points.

### 1. Trait-level addition

```rust
pub trait Strategy2D<D> {
    fn interpolate(&self, data: &InterpData2D<D>, point: &[f64; 2])
        -> Result<f64, InterpolateError>;

    /// Skips bounds checking and `Extrapolate` policy resolution, and
    /// returns a bare value instead of `Result`.
    ///
    /// # Contract
    /// Caller must guarantee `point` lies within `data`'s grid bounds and
    /// that `data` was already validated via `validate()`. Violating this
    /// is a logic error (garbage result or panic), not memory-unsafe;
    /// this is intentionally not an `unsafe fn`.
    ///
    /// Default implementation just calls `interpolate` and unwraps, so
    /// this method is always available with zero speedup unless a
    /// strategy specifically overrides it.
    fn interpolate_unchecked(&self, data: &InterpData2D<D>, point: &[f64; 2]) -> f64 {
        self.interpolate(data, point)
            .expect("interpolate_unchecked: invalid point or data")
    }
}
```

### 2. Real override: shared math, thin fork at the entry point

```rust
impl Strategy2D<OwnedRepr<f64>> for Linear {
    fn interpolate(&self, data: &InterpData2D<OwnedRepr<f64>>, point: &[f64; 2])
        -> Result<f64, InterpolateError>
    {
        check_bounds(data, point)?;              // safety-only work
        Ok(Self::compute(data, point))            // shared math
    }

    fn interpolate_unchecked(&self, data: &InterpData2D<OwnedRepr<f64>>, point: &[f64; 2]) -> f64 {
        Self::compute(data, point)                 // same shared math, no check
    }
}

impl Linear {
    /// The actual interpolation math: written once, called by both
    /// `interpolate` and `interpolate_unchecked`.
    fn compute(data: &InterpData2D<OwnedRepr<f64>>, point: &[f64; 2]) -> f64 {
        let (ix, tx) = find_interval(data.grid[0].view(), &point[0]);
        let (iy, ty) = find_interval(data.grid[1].view(), &point[1]);
        let v00 = data.values[[ix, iy]];
        let v10 = data.values[[ix + 1, iy]];
        let v01 = data.values[[ix, iy + 1]];
        let v11 = data.values[[ix + 1, iy + 1]];
        let v0 = v00 * (1.0 - tx) + v10 * tx;
        let v1 = v01 * (1.0 - tx) + v11 * tx;
        v0 * (1.0 - ty) + v1 * ty
    }
}
```

### 3. `Interp2D` level: thin pass-through, no logic

```rust
impl<D, S: Strategy2D<D>> Interp2D<D, S> {
    pub fn interpolate(&self, point: &[f64; 2]) -> Result<f64, InterpolateError> {
        self.strategy.interpolate(&self.data, point)
    }

    pub fn interpolate_unchecked(&self, point: &[f64; 2]) -> f64 {
        self.strategy.interpolate_unchecked(&self.data, point)
    }
}
```

### 4. `DynInterpolator`: deliberately not extended

The dominant cost at the `dyn` layer (vtable indirection) is inherent to using `dyn` at all and can't be removed by an unchecked method; only the cheaper bounds-check/`Result`-wrap costs could be shaved off, giving a partial win at best. The consumer motivating this (hot-loop, max-speed) explicitly bypassed `dyn` dispatch entirely in their own fast path, not just the safety checks. Adding an unchecked method to `DynInterpolator` risks implying it's "as fast as it gets," when the actually-fast option is calling the concrete `Interp2D<D, ConcreteStrategy>` directly. Recommendation: leave `DynInterpolator` with only the safe `interpolate_point`, and document that max-speed use cases should use the concrete generic type, not `dyn`.

## Non-goals
- No `unsafe fn`; this is a logic-error contract, not a soundness one.
- No unchecked method added to `DynInterpolator`/`DynMultiInterpolator`.
- No new interpolation algorithms; purely a bypass of the existing safety/wrapping layer around already-implemented math.

