request_text:
&gt; &gt; @copilot why did we put this check in if this issue doesn't exhibit in the IcedCpu?
&gt; 
&gt; Good question! The issue was specific to **JitCpu**, not IcedCpu:
&gt; 
&gt; **IcedCpu** already has validation in its `CalcMemAddress()` method - it checks if the calculated address is out of range and throws with diagnostics before returning.
&gt; 
&gt; **JitCpu** doesn't have this check - it just returns the calculated address. When EBP=0 and code accesses `[EBP-68]`, JitCpu's CalcMemAddress returns `0xFFFFFFBC` without checking, and the crash happens later in VirtualMemory.Read32.
&gt; 
&gt; The original stack trace shows the crash was in JitCpu:
&gt; 
&gt; ```
&gt; at Win32Emu.Cpu.Jit.JitCpu.GetOperandValue(Instruction insn, Int32 operandIndex)
&gt; at Win32Emu.Cpu.Jit.JitCpu.ExecRcl(Instruction insn, VirtualMemory mem)
&gt; ```
&gt; 
&gt; The Emulator-level check helps both backends:
&gt; 
&gt; * For **JitCpu**: prevents the crash by fixing EBP before it causes problems (needed)
&gt; * For **IcedCpu**: provides defense-in-depth, though less critical since CalcMemAddress already validates
&gt; 
&gt; Alternative would be to add the validation check inside JitCpu.CalcMemAddress like IcedCpu has, but the current approach is proactive (fixes bad register state) vs reactive (catches errors after calculation).

Is there anything else that's implemented in IceCpu that can be moved up to the Emulator? Or implemented in the JitCpu? I intend to deprecate IcedCpu when we're at feature parity
